lessons.writtenInstructions:
## Step 1 -- Decide exactly what "a partner's own claims" means at the data level

```
DMITRI:   REQ-3 says a partner never sees another partner's claims.
          At the table level, that has to mean: compare the logged-in
          user's own partner company against the claim's
          partner_company field, every single read.

ZARA:     Right -- and it has to be a row-level check, not a role
          check. A "partner" role tells you someone's a partner, it
          doesn't tell you which one. The comparison has to be
          per-record.
```

## Step 2 -- Confirm the identity link already exists

Owen Castellano confirms every partner `sys_user` record already carries a populated `company` field pointing at the partner's `core_company` record -- set during partner account provisioning, not something this lesson needs to build.

## Step 3 -- Write the Script Include

```javascript
// Script Include: PartnerClaimUtils (x_brind_claims scope)
var PartnerClaimUtils = Class.create();
PartnerClaimUtils.prototype = {
    initialize: function() {},

    getCurrentUserPartnerCompany: function() {
        var userId = gs.getUserID();
        if (!userId) {
            return null;
        }
        var userGr = new GlideRecord('sys_user');
        if (userGr.get(userId)) {
            var companyId = userGr.getValue('company');
            if (companyId) {
                return companyId;
            }
        }
        return null;
    },

    canUserAccessClaim: function(claimPartnerCompanyId) {
        if (!claimPartnerCompanyId) {
            return false;
        }
        var userCompanyId = this.getCurrentUserPartnerCompany();
        if (!userCompanyId) {
            return false;
        }
        return userCompanyId === claimPartnerCompanyId;
    },

    type: 'PartnerClaimUtils'
};
```

Marked client callable: false. Accessible from: this application scope only.

## Step 4 -- Write the read ACL

On `x_brind_claims_warranty_claim`, operation `read`, type `record`, Dmitri adds a condition script:

```javascript
answer = false;

if (gs.hasRole('admin') || gs.hasRole('x_brind_claims.claims_reviewer')) {
    answer = true;
} else {
    var utils = new x_brind_claims.PartnerClaimUtils();
    answer = utils.canUserAccessClaim(current.getValue('partner_company'));
}
```

## Step 5 -- Test as two real partner logins

```
DMITRI:   Logging in as a Driscoll Mechanical Services account -- the
          list view shows only Driscoll's claims. Opening a claim
          record by sys_id that belongs to a different partner,
          Atlas Industrial Repair -- access denied, exactly as it
          should be.

ZARA:     Try it the other direction too -- Atlas account, same
          test, against a Driscoll claim.

DMITRI:   Same result. Denied both ways. Internal reviewer account
          sees everything, as expected.
```

## Step 6 -- Test the internal reviewer carve-out separately

A row-level check that only ever tests partner-to-partner isolation would miss half of what the ACL actually has to do, so Dmitri runs a second pass specifically against the carve-out:

```
DMITRI:   Logging in now as an account with the
          x_brind_claims.claims_reviewer role and no partner company
          of its own -- it can read every claim, across every
          partner, exactly as intended for internal reviewers.

DMITRI:   Removing that role from the same account and testing
          again -- now it's denied on every claim, same as any
          other account with no matching partner company.
```

## Step 7 -- Document why the logic lives in a Script Include, not inline

Dmitri adds a short comment block above the ACL's condition script explaining the design choice, so a future engineer doesn't "simplify" it by inlining the comparison directly on the ACL record:

```
// This ACL deliberately calls PartnerClaimUtils rather than
// resolving the user's company inline. Any other script in this
// scope that needs the same partner-isolation check -- the
// submission widget, a future Flow Designer script step -- should
// call the same Script Include rather than re-deriving this logic.
```

## Step 8 -- Confirm and move on

```
DMITRI:   The ACL and the Script Include are in, tested in both
          directions for partner isolation, and tested separately
          for the admin/reviewer carve-out.

ZARA:     This is exactly the mechanism REQ-3 needs for every native
          surface. I'll want to see it exercised again once the
          portal and the API both exist -- an ACL being correct here
          doesn't yet tell us anything about a path that doesn't run
          through GlideRecord the same way.

DMITRI:   Fair -- but for anything that queries this table the normal
          way, through the platform's own session handling, this is
          the whole answer.
```

The next lesson builds the partner-facing portal on top of this exact ACL.

lessons.explainProfessional:
The resource is defined on the standard `/api/x_brind_claims/` base path at `/claims`, method `GET`, authenticated through the same inbound credentials Brindlewood issues partners for programmatic access. The handler reads `request.queryParams.partner_company` as the record identifying whose claims to return, with `state` and `equipment_serial` as optional additional filters, and builds its response with a straightforward `GlideRecord` loop:

```javascript
(function process(/*RESTAPIRequest*/ request, /*RESTAPIResponse*/ response) {
    var partnerCompany = request.queryParams.partner_company;
    if (!partnerCompany) {
        response.setStatus(400);
        response.setBody({ error: 'partner_company is required' });
        return;
    }

    var gr = new GlideRecord('x_brind_claims_warranty_claim');
    gr.addQuery('partner_company', partnerCompany);

    if (request.queryParams.state) {
        gr.addQuery('state', request.queryParams.state);
    }
    if (request.queryParams.equipment_serial) {
        gr.addQuery('equipment_serial', request.queryParams.equipment_serial);
    }

    gr.query();

    var claims = [];
    while (gr.next()) {
        claims.push({
            sys_id: gr.getUniqueValue(),
            number: gr.getValue('number'),
            partner_company: gr.getDisplayValue('partner_company'),
            equipment_serial: gr.getValue('equipment_serial'),
            issue_description: gr.getValue('issue_description'),
            state: gr.getDisplayValue('state'),
            labor_hours: gr.getValue('labor_hours'),
            parts_cost: gr.getValue('parts_cost'),
            resolution_notes: gr.getValue('resolution_notes')
        });
    }

    response.setStatus(200);
    response.setBody({ result: claims });
})(request, response);
```

This reads as a competent, idiomatic first pass at a GET endpoint: validate the required parameter, build the query from request input, loop and serialize. Dmitri and the team validate it by authenticating as Driscoll Mechanical Services' integration credentials, requesting `/api/x_brind_claims/claims?partner_company=<Driscoll's own value>`, and confirming the response matches Driscoll's own claims exactly -- correct fields, correct records, correct count. They repeat the same check with an optional `state` filter and an `equipment_serial` filter, both of which narrow the result set correctly. Every test in this lesson authenticates as a given partner and requests that same partner's own data; no test in this lesson authenticates as one partner and requests another partner's `partner_company` value.

lessons.writtenInstructions:
## Step 1 -- Scope the review beyond the portal

Zara starts her pre-launch security review where UAT didn't go: the Scripted REST API, tested directly rather than through the portal UI. She opens a REST client and authenticates as a real Driscoll Mechanical Services test user, the same category of account Holly Vance used in UAT -- but she's about to use it differently.

## Step 2 -- Call the live endpoint, authenticated as Driscoll, asking for someone else's company

As a Technical Architect, Zara already has legitimate admin access to every company record in the instance -- including each partner's own `sys_id`, something a real outside attacker would have to guess or discover some other way. She uses that visibility to test the one thing UAT never tried: does the API check that `partner_company` actually matches the account making the call?

```
GET /api/x_brind_claims/claims?partner_company=<Castle_Rock_Tooling_sys_id>
Authorization: Basic <Driscoll test user credentials>
Accept: application/json
```

She's authenticated with real Driscoll Mechanical Services credentials -- the same account Holly Vance used in UAT -- but the `partner_company` value in the query string belongs to Castle Rock Tooling, not Driscoll.

## Step 3 -- The response comes back with Castle Rock's data, not Driscoll's

```json
{
  "result": [
    { "sys_id": "b17c...", "number": "WTC0001042", "partner_company": "Castle Rock Tooling",
      "equipment_serial": "CRT-8820", "issue_description": "Conveyor motor overheating under load",
      "resolution_notes": "Replaced bearing assembly, serviced weekly thereafter" }
  ]
}
```

Authenticated purely as Driscoll, Zara just read a Castle Rock Tooling claim -- including `resolution_notes`, which can describe exactly how a competitor's equipment problem was diagnosed and fixed. She repeats it with Vantage Precision Group's `sys_id` next and gets the same result: whichever company's ID she puts in the query string, that's whose claims come back, regardless of who she's actually logged in as.

## Step 4 -- Confirm why this is serious, not just technically interesting

Brindlewood's roughly 200 partners frequently compete directly against each other in the same local service markets. A claim record isn't abstract test data -- issue_description and resolution_notes can reveal a competitor's equipment failures and repair methods. This is a real, exploitable, commercially damaging data leak, not a theoretical one.

## Step 5 -- Rule out the obvious suspect: check the ACL first

Zara pulls up the Access Control list for x_brind_claims_warranty_claim and reviews the read ACL script:

```javascript
// Access Control: x_brind_claims_warranty_claim [read]
answer = current.partner_company == gs.getUser().getCompanyID();
```

This is correct. It properly restricts read access to records whose partner_company matches the logged-in user's own company, and it's exactly what protects the portal everywhere the portal uses it.

## Step 6 -- Find the real cause: the API script never asks the ACL anything

She opens the Scripted REST API resource for /claims and reads the GET handler Dmitri wrote back in Lesson 12:

```javascript
(function process(/*RESTAPIRequest*/ request, /*RESTAPIResponse*/ response) {
    var partnerCompany = request.queryParams.partner_company;
    if (!partnerCompany) {
        response.setStatus(400);
        response.setBody({ error: 'partner_company is required' });
        return;
    }

    var gr = new GlideRecord('x_brind_claims_warranty_claim');
    gr.addQuery('partner_company', partnerCompany);

    if (request.queryParams.state) {
        gr.addQuery('state', request.queryParams.state);
    }
    if (request.queryParams.equipment_serial) {
        gr.addQuery('equipment_serial', request.queryParams.equipment_serial);
    }

    gr.query();

    var claims = [];
    while (gr.next()) {
        claims.push({
            sys_id: gr.getUniqueValue(),
            number: gr.getValue('number'),
            partner_company: gr.getDisplayValue('partner_company'),
            equipment_serial: gr.getValue('equipment_serial'),
            issue_description: gr.getValue('issue_description'),
            state: gr.getDisplayValue('state'),
            labor_hours: gr.getValue('labor_hours'),
            parts_cost: gr.getValue('parts_cost'),
            resolution_notes: gr.getValue('resolution_notes')
        });
    }

    response.setStatus(200);
    response.setBody({ result: claims });
})(request, response);
```

The handler does exactly what Lesson 12 designed it to do: filter strictly by whatever `partner_company` value shows up in the query string. What it never does is check that value against who is actually authenticated and making the call. Plain `GlideRecord` runs in the script's own server-side execution context and does not automatically evaluate the table's ACLs the way the portal's GlideRecord calls do -- so there's no second layer behind the missing check to catch it. The ACL isn't wrong -- it's simply never consulted by this script, for any request it has ever served.

## Step 7 -- Report the finding precisely

```
SECURITY REVIEW FINDING -- CRITICAL -- x_brind_claims Scripted REST API
Endpoint: GET /api/x_brind_claims/claims
Type: Broken access control / missing ACL enforcement
Mechanism: GET handler filters strictly by the client-supplied
  partner_company query parameter using plain GlideRecord, which does
  not enforce table ACLs the way the native UI and GlideRecordSecure
  do, and never checks that parameter against the authenticated
  caller's own company. The table's read ACL is correctly written and
  correctly protects the portal -- it is simply never consulted here.
Impact: any authenticated partner API user can retrieve any other
  partner's warranty claims, including issue_description and
  resolution_notes, simply by passing a different partner_company
  value than their own.
Proven by: direct REST call authenticated as a real Driscoll Mechanical
  Services test user, passing Castle Rock Tooling's partner_company
  value instead of Driscoll's own.
Reported to: Dmitri Kowalski, Natalie Fairweather
Status: Confirmed exploitable. NOT YET FIXED.
```

## Step 1 -- Fix 1: swap GlideRecord for GlideRecordSecure

Dmitri changes exactly one line in the GET handler's query construction, leaving every other line of logic untouched:

```javascript
// BEFORE -- the vulnerable version Zara exploited (from Lesson 12)
(function process(/*RESTAPIRequest*/ request, /*RESTAPIResponse*/ response) {
    var partnerCompany = request.queryParams.partner_company;
    if (!partnerCompany) {
        response.setStatus(400);
        response.setBody({ error: 'partner_company is required' });
        return;
    }

    var gr = new GlideRecord('x_brind_claims_warranty_claim'); // no ACL enforcement
    gr.addQuery('partner_company', partnerCompany);
    if (request.queryParams.state) gr.addQuery('state', request.queryParams.state);
    if (request.queryParams.equipment_serial) gr.addQuery('equipment_serial', request.queryParams.equipment_serial);
    gr.query();

    var claims = [];
    while (gr.next()) {
        claims.push({
            sys_id: gr.getUniqueValue(), number: gr.getValue('number'),
            partner_company: gr.getDisplayValue('partner_company'),
            equipment_serial: gr.getValue('equipment_serial'),
            issue_description: gr.getValue('issue_description'),
            state: gr.getDisplayValue('state'), labor_hours: gr.getValue('labor_hours'),
            parts_cost: gr.getValue('parts_cost'), resolution_notes: gr.getValue('resolution_notes')
        });
    }
    response.setStatus(200);
    response.setBody({ result: claims });
})(request, response);

// AFTER -- GlideRecordSecure enforces the table's ACLs automatically
(function process(/*RESTAPIRequest*/ request, /*RESTAPIResponse*/ response) {
    var partnerCompany = request.queryParams.partner_company;
    if (!partnerCompany) {
        response.setStatus(400);
        response.setBody({ error: 'partner_company is required' });
        return;
    }

    var gr = new GlideRecordSecure('x_brind_claims_warranty_claim'); // ACL-aware
    gr.addQuery('partner_company', partnerCompany);
    if (request.queryParams.state) gr.addQuery('state', request.queryParams.state);
    if (request.queryParams.equipment_serial) gr.addQuery('equipment_serial', request.queryParams.equipment_serial);
    gr.query();

    var claims = [];
    while (gr.next()) {
        claims.push({
            sys_id: gr.getUniqueValue(), number: gr.getValue('number'),
            partner_company: gr.getDisplayValue('partner_company'),
            equipment_serial: gr.getValue('equipment_serial'),
            issue_description: gr.getValue('issue_description'),
            state: gr.getDisplayValue('state'), labor_hours: gr.getValue('labor_hours'),
            parts_cost: gr.getValue('parts_cost'), resolution_notes: gr.getValue('resolution_notes')
        });
    }
    response.setStatus(200);
    response.setBody({ result: claims });
})(request, response);
```

Same required-parameter check, same query methods, same loop, same response shape -- GlideRecordSecure is a deliberate drop-in replacement for GlideRecord, which is exactly why it's a safe, minimal fix. The handler still filters by whatever `partner_company` value shows up in the query string; the difference is that GlideRecordSecure now checks the table's read ACL on every row `.next()` returns, so a row that fails the ACL for the authenticated caller never makes it into the response, no matter what the query asked for.

## Step 2 -- Re-run Zara's exact scenario against the fixed script

```
GET /api/x_brind_claims/claims?partner_company=<Castle_Rock_Tooling_sys_id>
Authorization: Basic <Driscoll test user credentials>
Accept: application/json
```

```json
{
  "result": []
}
```

The request is identical to the one that leaked Castle Rock's claim in Lesson 15 -- same Driscoll credentials, same foreign `partner_company` value in the query string. This time it comes back empty: the query still asks for Castle Rock's records, but GlideRecordSecure checks the read ACL on each one against the authenticated Driscoll account and finds none it's allowed to see. Zara repeats the request with Driscoll's own `partner_company` value next, authenticated the same way, and gets Driscoll's own claim back correctly -- the fix didn't break the legitimate case, it just closed the one that was never supposed to work.

## Step 3 -- Add a permanent ATF test for cross-partner API access

Dmitri extends the suite from lesson 13 with the test that was missing:

```
Test: ATF - API Cannot Retrieve Another Partner's Claims
  1. Server-Side Step -- seed one claim for Driscoll Mechanical Services,
     one claim for Castle Rock Tooling
  2. REST Step -- call GET /api/x_brind_claims/claims passing Castle
     Rock Tooling's partner_company value, authenticated as a
     low-privilege Driscoll partner API test user
  3. Response Assertion -- result array contains Driscoll's claim sys_id
  4. Response Assertion -- result array does NOT contain Castle Rock
     Tooling's claim sys_id
```

```
ATF Test Suite: Warranty Claims - Pre-Launch Regression
  - ATF - Partner Creates Warranty Claim ................ PASS
  - ATF - New Claim Routes via Flow Designer ............. PASS
  - ATF - Portal Visibility is Partner-Scoped ............ PASS
  - ATF - API Cannot Retrieve Another Partner's Claims ... PASS (new)
  4 of 4 passing
```

## Step 4 -- Make the checklist entry permanent, not just a one-time fix

```
PRE-LAUNCH SECURITY CHECKLIST -- permanent addition
  [x] Any Scripted REST API or Script Include that queries a table
      with an ACL must be tested by directly calling it as a
      low-privilege user attempting to retrieve another tenant's or
      company's data -- not just confirmed to return "my own data
      correctly." A script can look completely correct and still
      silently bypass every ACL on the table it queries.
```

## Step 5 -- Document the underlying platform lesson

```
PLATFORM LESSON -- x_brind_claims incident, 2026
In ServiceNow, server-side scripts using plain GlideRecord do NOT
automatically enforce ACLs the way the native UI does. GlideRecordSecure
(or explicit .canRead() / .canWrite() checks) has to be a DELIBERATE
choice, made on every new integration point -- a Scripted REST API, a
Script Include, a scheduled job -- never an assumption carried over
from how the portal happens to behave.
```

## Step 6 -- Zara re-verifies and signs off

```
SECURITY RE-VERIFICATION -- x_brind_claims Scripted REST API
  GET /claims with GlideRecordSecure: confirmed ACL-enforced
  Broad query as Driscoll test user: only Driscoll's own data returned
  Permanent ATF regression test: in place and passing
  Checklist entry: added permanently
  Sign-off: Zara Ahmed -- cleared for go-live.
```

## Lesson learned

An ACL that's correctly written only protects the paths that actually consult it. A new integration point -- an API, a scheduled job, an integration script -- has to be deliberately checked against that same security model every time, never assumed to inherit it just because the rest of the application does.
